  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through next Thursday...\n\nNorthwest flow will continue Thursday bringing chances (30-60%) \nfor scattered showers and thunderstorms north and east of\nBillings. Severe storms are not expected.\n\nMore active weather is expected Saturday and Sunday. A splitting \ntrough will create low pressure over the Central Plains and the \nMontana/Canada border. These low pressure systems will create a \nstrong conveyer belt of moisture from the Gulf. Ensemble systems \nare indicating PWAT anomalies near 1.4 in Southeast Montana. \nSoundings from global models are not showing indicators for severe\nweather with upper level capping and limited shear. Showers and \ngeneral thunderstorms are favored at this time. The highest \nprecipitation amounts will be far to our south and east but parts \nof southeast Montana should still receive beneficial rain. The \nBeartooth/Absaroka Mountains and locations east of Billings have \nthe best chances (60-90%) for precipitation. The \nBeartooth/Absaroka Mountains and far Southeast Montana have the \nbest chance (50-70%) at seeing greater than 0.5 inches of \nprecipitation. \n\nLingering precipitation chances (20-50%) are possible Monday and \nTuesday as the 500 mb trough to our north strengthens and moves \nsouth. Seasonal temperatures are expected over the next week with\ntemperatures in the 70s to low 80s F. Torgerson",
